    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nFlat-12 Awesomeness: 1970 Porsche 917K Parades Gulf Livery at Imola\n13 photos\nPhoto: 19Bozzy92/YouTube\nThere's no denying that Porsche has built some of the greatest race cars out there. Likewise, there's no denying that the Germans have a very long list of successful motorsport rigs. It all started with the 356 and 550 Spyder in the 1950s and still goes on with the 963 and 911.\nPorsche has dominated various racing series over the years, but it's been particularly successful on the endurance scene. The Germans have the most wins at the 24 Hours of Le Mans, 19, with the next automaker boasting only 13 victories. These wins were achieved between 1970 and 2017.\nNo fewer than eight Porsches took the checkered flag first at Circuit de la Sarthe. The 919 Hybrid won in the 2010s, while the 911 GT1 and WSC-95 scored wins in the 1990s. The 1980s were literally dominated by the 956 and 962, while the 936 and 935 won the event in the second half of the 1970s.\nBut I'm here to talk about Porsche's first Le Mans winner, the 917 . And that's mainly because I also want to show you footage of the 917K that was spotted at a vintage racing event at Imola. It's also wearing the iconic Gulf livery.\nThe 917 was born in the late 1960s, following a regulation change that limited Group 6 engine displacement to 3.0 liters. At the same time, the Commission Sportive Internationale allowed Group 4 sports cars to complete using engines of up to 5.0 liters. However, these cars had to be street-legal and produced in at least 50 units (later dropped to 25).\nUnable to defeat the Ford GT40 with a 907 racer powered by a 3.0-liter flat-eight in 1968, Porsche set out to create a Group 4 racer. With only a few months to spare until the start of the 1969 season, Porsche tasked the legendary Hans Mezger with building a new flat-12 powerplant. The resulting 4.5-liter mill delivered around 550 horsepower.\nPorsche then built a brand-new car around that engine. And it managed to produce 25 units in just ten months, in time for the homologation deadline. Lightweight and aerodynamic, the 917 was capable of speeds in excess of 200 mph (322 kph). However, both cars that entered the 1969 24 Hours of Le Mans failed to finish.\nNext year, Porsche returned with a revised, short-tail version of the racer. The 917K debuted at Circuit de la Sarthe, won the race, and then went on to win six of the remaining nine events of the endurance championship. A revised version won Porsche's second Le Mans in 1971.\nAlthough the 917K was retired after the 1971 season, Porsche continued to race other iterations of the car through 1973. The Can-Am-spec 917/30 is perhaps the most radical race car ever created thanks to a twin-turbocharged engine good for up to 1,500 horsepower.\nThe car you see here did not win at Le Mans, but it's an authentic 917K and sports one of the most iconic liveries out there: the blue-and-orange of Gulf Oil . The vehicle appears to be chassis 014, which made its debut at the 24 Hours of Daytona. Driven by Jo Siffert and Brian Redman, the Porsche finished in second place. The car was also raced at Spa and Watkins Glen that year.\nThe car belongs to a Japanese collector now, and I bet it spends most of its time in storage. Fortunately, the owner agreed to have his 917K paraded at Imola so we can hear its vintage flat-12 powerplant scream. Yup, it's one of those videos you need to watch with the volume cranked up.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nPorsche Porsche 917 Porsche 917K classic car race car Imola Le Mans\nAbout the author: Ciprian Florea\nAsk Ciprian about cars and he'll reveal an obsession with classics and an annoyance with modern design cues. Read his articles and you'll understand why his ideal SUV is the 1969 Chevrolet K5 Blazer. Full profile\nUP NEXT Canadian 1971 Corvette 454 Rocks Holy Grail LS6 and Automatic Combo, It's Rarer Than Dragon Eggs",
